Most gardeners think of diascias as low-growing bedding plants suited to a summer container or the front of a sunny border. However, there is a much larger group of diascias than is generally appreciated. Indeed, there are around 30 species, some of which grow in wild in the south of Europe, and several of these can be used in the garden.
The most common species is D. cordata, with its silvery, silver-grey, lance-shaped leaves and pinkish flowers in late spring or early summer. It is a popular garden plant in the UK, although it can be fussy about its soil and position, and is often not as successful as it could be. The other common garden diascia is D. fruticosa, also known as D. alba. This is a much smaller plant, with grey-green, oval leaves and white flowers, and is more tolerant of the UK climate, although it is still a bit of a fussy plant.
